<%--
Code_Input - a numeric code typed one digit per box

The row of boxes a one-time code is typed into: one digit each, the focus advancing as you
type and retreating as you delete. It is a form input like any other - ONE named value, the
digits entered so far as a string ("" when empty, "4071" part-way, "407193" complete).

    <Form_Field $label="Verification code" $required=true>
        <Code_Input $name="code" />
    </Form_Field>

$name      - the field name (form contract).
$length    - how many digits (default 6).
$align     - "center" centres the row in its container; omitted, it sits at the start.
$disabled  - disables every box; the value is still reported.

Behaviour, all of it in Code_Input.js:
  - digits only. Any other character is removed as it is typed or pasted.
  - the boxes always fill from the left with no gaps, so wherever you click or tab in, the
    cursor lands in the first empty box - or after the digit in the last box when all are full.
  - pasting (or a phone's one-time-code autofill) into any box spreads the digits across the
    boxes that follow; anything past the last box is discarded.
  - Backspace clears the previous box when the current one is empty.
  - Enter is left alone once every box is filled, so it takes the path Enter takes in any text
    input (a form's submit, a form dialog's primary button). With boxes still empty it is
    swallowed.

Methods:
  val() / val(value)  - the form-input contract. Setting takes the digits of whatever it is
                        given; in practice it is called with '' to clear.
  focus()             - the base class's; it lands where the next digit goes.
  is_complete()       - true when every box holds a digit.

There is NO "you must enter all N digits" validation here. Completeness is a rule the SERVER
can see for itself, so it lives there once - a client copy of it would hide the day the
server's copy went missing (see Rsx_Form's docblock). is_complete() is for a host that wants
to know, and the Enter rule is interaction design, not a check.

Each box is a Bootstrap .form-control, so border, focus ring, disabled and invalid states are
the application's own. An application restyles the widget with its own rules on .Code_Input.
--%>
<Define:Code_Input extends="Form_Input_Abstract" tag="div">
    <%
        const _length = int(this.args.length) || 6;
        let _class = 'Code_Input__boxes';
        if (this.args.align === 'center') {
            _class += ' Code_Input__boxes--center';
        }
    %>
    <div class="<%= _class %>">
        <% for (let _i = 0; _i < _length; _i++) { %>
            <%-- No maxlength: a paste has to arrive whole before it can be spread across the
                 boxes. Only the first box asks for the one-time-code autofill, so a phone
                 offers the code once and it lands where distribution starts. --%>
            <input type="text" class="form-control Code_Input__box"
                   inputmode="numeric"
                   aria-label="Digit <%= _i + 1 %> of <%= _length %>"
                   autocomplete="<%= _i === 0 ? 'one-time-code' : 'off' %>"
                   <% if (this.args.disabled) { %>disabled<% } %> />
        <% } %>
    </div>
</Define:Code_Input>
